description:
crate-jmap-tasks-server/src/backend.rs:128-139:

    /// Returns \`true\` if this backend enforces the \`isDraft\` immutability invariant
    /// atomically in \`update_object\` (by returning \`SetError { error_type: InvalidProperties,
    /// properties: [\"isDraft\"] }\` when a patch attempts to set \`isDraft: true\` on a
    /// published task).
    ///
    /// When \`true\`, the handler skips the \`get_objects\` pre-fetch in \`Task/set\` update
    /// processing, saving one backend round-trip per update that contains \`isDraft: true\`.
    ///
    /// Default: \`false\` — pre-fetch is always performed.
    fn enforce_is_draft_atomically(&self) -> bool {
        false
    }

The docstring frames this as a *performance* knob (\"saving one backend round-trip\"). It is not. It is a correctness-handover contract:

  - When the method returns false: handler in task.rs:217-247 does a get_objects pre-fetch, inspects the current isDraft, and rejects false→true reverts. Backend never sees the bad patch.

  - When the method returns true: handler skips the pre-fetch (task.rs:217-219 — the && !backend.enforce_is_draft_atomically() short-circuit). The patch is forwarded to update_object. If update_object DOES NOT actually validate isDraft atomically, the false→true revert succeeds silently. A consumer reading the docstring as a perf flag will flip it to true without realising they have just committed to enforcing a draft-tasks-06 §4 (lines 733-737) invariant themselves. Silent data corruption results.

The framing problem is real because:

  1. The doc says \"saving one backend round-trip per update\" — that is the consumer's incentive to flip it.
  2. The doc does NOT say \"MUST return SetError { invalidProperties: [isDraft] } from update_object when the patch attempts to set isDraft:true on a published task; failure to do so will silently violate draft-tasks-06 §4 immutability\".
  3. There is no test in this crate that asserts a backend returning true here actually does enforce. A consumer can ship a buggy production backend that passes every test in jmap-tasks-server's own suite.

Three viable contract-strengthening fixes, in increasing order of safety:

  - Doc fix: rewrite the rustdoc as a correctness contract, with a Safety section equivalent (\"If you return true and your update_object does not atomically reject draft-revert patches, your backend silently violates spec invariant draft-tasks-06 §4. This is an opt-in correctness handover, not a performance toggle.\")

  - Behaviour fix: rename to enforce_is_draft_atomically (kept) and add a debug-only assert path — when this method returns true and dev-mode integration tests run, the handler verifies via a probe update_object call that the backend rejects the revert.

  - Type fix: replace the bool flag with a named type alias of the SetError SHAPE the backend returns, so the contract is type-system-enforced. E.g. fn isdraft_enforcement_strategy() -> IsDraftEnforcementStrategy { Handler } with explicit Backend(BackendStrategy { ... }) variant.

A 0.x crate can still afford the rename. Once a real third-party consumer flips this bit to true and ships, the spec-violation footgun is permanent.
